{"title":"2017 USAMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Evan_Chen,_Calvin_Deng)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Evan Chen, Calvin Deng)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(INCOMPLETE)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (INCOMPLETE)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/d/75d4a3d7f9e00ba4ad4d80289be98fe888e31448.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{Z}$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> denote the set of all integers. Find all real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/c/40c60c289e0343a236653229b2132befdfeabd04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> such that there exists a labeling of the lattice points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bff28acc1833d92da131e793f36a99ac614a816.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$( x, y ) \\in \\mathbf{Z}^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"19\" /> with positive integers for which: only finitely many distinct labels occur, and for each label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, the distance between any two points labeled <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/f/0dffdaf42ac3fcc014af64f984a34b3e2ad9519c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c^i$\" width=\"12\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Evan_Chen.2C_Calvin_Deng.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Evan_Chen,_Calvin_Deng)\">Solution 1 (Evan Chen, Calvin Deng)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Evan Chen, Calvin Deng)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>See page 11 of this PDF:\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://web.evanchen.cc/exams/USAMO-2017-notes.pdf\">https://web.evanchen.cc/exams/USAMO-2017-notes.pdf</a> \n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28INCOMPLETE.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(INCOMPLETE)\">Solution 2 (INCOMPLETE)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (INCOMPLETE)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/c/7eca19e3a959f73abc127a2db67def0bf6ca62ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\le 1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" /> we can label every lattice point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cdd1b53e5faf7a118222e4b7571d1e9afa0cf56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/a/06a87eee69bf1c0028ccb3e691f621ce4f62698f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\le \\sqrt[4]{2},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"20\" /> we can make a \"checkerboard\" labeling, i.e. label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/f/adfd9ae8a3fac031d3b8b470a52a709a23d4d6d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/d/d3da9f8c7fe0473fdd6f8597eff61d71fedda430.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"16\" /> is even and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/d/d3da9f8c7fe0473fdd6f8597eff61d71fedda430.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"16\" /> is odd. One can easily verify that these labelings satisfy the required conditions. Therefore, a labeling as desired exists for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/6/5d6803b29ca5e78f32e108cb4e0e00f300e1e668.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; c\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"94\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>An iterated version of the checkerboard labeling can actually work for all values <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/094a237e9c2b302b2a74e85128c9e9ea4a83be43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c &lt; \\sqrt{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> For convenience, define the original lattice grid to be the set of all lattice points in the coordinate plane. Define a modified lattice grid of size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> to be a structure similar to the lattice points on the coordinate plane, but with the minimum separation between any two points equaling <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> (as opposed to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />).\n</p><p>On the first step, assign a label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to half of the points in a checkerboard arrangement. One can see that the points that have not yet been labeled form a modified lattice grid of size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/4/2c4dc1409208ff6a3039f4871d85c16763334983.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" /> (this lattice grid is also rotated by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/1/821d3f11ab7a3e93c7a404a34224228f0449505a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> from the original lattice grid). At this point, for the second step, assign a label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to half of the points, again in a checkerboard arrangement. At this point, the points that have not yet been labeled form a modified lattice grid of size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (and again, it is rotated <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/1/821d3f11ab7a3e93c7a404a34224228f0449505a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> from the modified lattice grid after the first step). One then continues in this fashion. For the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/0/930d97db6127d6f8a79cb7691ff60f1256458fa1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N^{\\text{th}}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> step, the points we are labeling are separated by at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/ccc4861e30f3d6834c07cf75a672be0de408d2d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}\\times\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)^{N-1} = \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)^N &gt; c^N,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"252\" height=\"35\" /> so we know that our labeling at each step is acceptable.\n</p><p>After the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/0/930d97db6127d6f8a79cb7691ff60f1256458fa1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N^{\\text{th}}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> step (where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is a natural number), the points that have not yet been labeled form a modified lattice grid with size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/c/04c2d71bdd8866d452cbdfc7b4422f73762ce998.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)^N.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"64\" height=\"35\" /> Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/7/5f7f6ef7ef93e1bb674a350b342e7d1afcda2747.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c &lt; \\sqrt{2},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" /> we will eventually have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/5/1355f13079f7dbb373f3b94f509b36d0bc241f43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)^N &gt; c^{N+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"121\" height=\"38\" /> for some sufficiently large <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b4a6d41204181432504f1108ded79e4a49e0e03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N.$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> At this point, we can label all remaining points in the original lattice grid <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/f/4ef4b3717e3db66a636476b06314ba6b40f916c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N+1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" /> and this produces a labeling of all of the lattice points in the plane that satisfies all of the conditions. Therefore, a labeling as desired exists for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/094a237e9c2b302b2a74e85128c9e9ea4a83be43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c &lt; \\sqrt{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We now prove that no labeling as desired exists for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/1/f91ccda5525fe7aa5fb42d4a3dc47c94c7f9e951.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\ge 2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> To do this, we will prove that labeling a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767b7dc4a8df95a9f8a0faa3bb155e7b49943407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" />-by-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767b7dc4a8df95a9f8a0faa3bb155e7b49943407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" /> square grid of lattice points requires at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/3/1030ffa4253096c8854b16b6102758ed9eb97cf2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> distinct labels for all natural numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />; hence for a sufficiently large section of the lattice plane the number of distinct labels required grows arbitrarily large, so the entire lattice plane cannot be labeled with finitely many distinct labels. We will prove this using induction.\n</p><p>For the base case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/7/39744f1e901ee42463a7548466b48204efd83627.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"47\" height=\"16\" /> we have four points in a square of side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cdd1b53e5faf7a118222e4b7571d1e9afa0cf56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> The maximum distance between any two of these points is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/1/df1cf9643ecdf02e1f635f624c5f0cd45f9997d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2} &lt; c^1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/6/0b68e446496dd8601f0cafb519ca46c86950720b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\ge 2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" /> so all four points must have different labels. This completes the base case.\n</p><p>Now, for the inductive step, suppose that labeling a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767b7dc4a8df95a9f8a0faa3bb155e7b49943407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" />-by-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767b7dc4a8df95a9f8a0faa3bb155e7b49943407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" /> square grid of lattice points requires at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/3/1030ffa4253096c8854b16b6102758ed9eb97cf2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> distinct labels for some natural number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d48e0968a64d782fe75bca620ce749938b7d0bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k.$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> We will now prove that labeling a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473b67382183fc823e117858fef19aaf2ec23948.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{k+1}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" />-by-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473b67382183fc823e117858fef19aaf2ec23948.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{k+1}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" /> square grid of lattice points requires at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/b/5db5481edd194e5c1ab919607ccf25b8719cefc6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k+4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" /> distinct labels.\n</p><p>Take a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473b67382183fc823e117858fef19aaf2ec23948.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{k+1}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" />-by-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473b67382183fc823e117858fef19aaf2ec23948.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{k+1}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" /> square grid of lattice points. Divide this grid into four quadrants, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/6/946d1d9e259a50091bdfdc7dcdb215da000a197c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"62\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/1/c61f1e8be9d73f590167b39417e42bbaa1787fe0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D.$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> By the inductive hypothesis, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> requires at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/3/1030ffa4253096c8854b16b6102758ed9eb97cf2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> distinct labels. At least one of these labels must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/3/1030ffa4253096c8854b16b6102758ed9eb97cf2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> or greater; take one such label and call it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/0/ab0bb0171685e5101725bf6851aaf364fb719639.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>The largest distance between any two points in the entire grid is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/f/c5f35f7fe1672ddb4fc45143d440a6c43267461a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}\\left(2^{k+1} - 1\\right) &lt; c^{k+3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"164\" height=\"23\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/1/f91ccda5525fe7aa5fb42d4a3dc47c94c7f9e951.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\ge 2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> Therefore, the label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> cannot be used anywhere else in the grid. However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/6/716f38839110cdec891c16874f0d7e337a1751fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B, C,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> each require at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/3/1030ffa4253096c8854b16b6102758ed9eb97cf2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> distinct labels as well by the inductive hypothesis. Thus, they must use at least one label that is not used in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/8207b79435a4bd499da7598fb44424c8a8499238.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> It follows that the entire grid requires at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/b/5db5481edd194e5c1ab919607ccf25b8719cefc6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k+4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" /> distinct labels. This completes the inductive step, and thus we conclude that no labeling as desired exists for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/1/f91ccda5525fe7aa5fb42d4a3dc47c94c7f9e951.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\ge 2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>I have heard from others that the actual boundary is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb485bd3ce593662cabace2375882fc28b737206.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> This makes intuitive sense, since the iterated checkerboard labeling outlined above just breaks down at this value (you will be able to get closer and closer to labeling all of the lattice points, but you can never get there, since you will never have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/5/1355f13079f7dbb373f3b94f509b36d0bc241f43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)^N &gt; c^{N+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"121\" height=\"38\" />). The inductive argument above seems fairly loose, so I think that it can be sharpened to bring the upper bound down to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/5/505ee066b7bae0313a6d0a6f974e588e46cd5263.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"20\" /> but I am not sure yet how exactly to do so. I think the way to do it is to somehow force <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> new labels (instead of just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />) each time you double the side length of the square grid.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We claim that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/a/4fa5dca8cbf3ead966309f01942a73e0f57cabb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c&lt;\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"21\" />, there exists such a configuration. \n</p><p>Lemma 1a: If there exists a configuration for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6f6e04c04b95f36f4499cd645fb363540fb86a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />, then for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/4/b24615662d7f8e3476c7768843c0e8c24fa60b08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_2\\le{c_1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" />, there also exists a configuration for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5a94f54f83d0d2dc88f527c47db299a61ffcfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />.\nNote that any labeling that can be done for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6f6e04c04b95f36f4499cd645fb363540fb86a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" /> exists for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5a94f54f83d0d2dc88f527c47db299a61ffcfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />. Thus, we may copy the configuration from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6f6e04c04b95f36f4499cd645fb363540fb86a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5a94f54f83d0d2dc88f527c47db299a61ffcfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />. \n</p><p>Lemma 1b: If there does not exist a configuration for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6f6e04c04b95f36f4499cd645fb363540fb86a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />, then for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/c/d4cf4c78332a4c779335ea732695078fd36d7af2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_2\\ge{c_1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" />, there does not exist a configuration for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5a94f54f83d0d2dc88f527c47db299a61ffcfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />.\nNote that any labeling which may be done for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5a94f54f83d0d2dc88f527c47db299a61ffcfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" /> should also be available for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6f6e04c04b95f36f4499cd645fb363540fb86a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />. However, we already stated that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6f6e04c04b95f36f4499cd645fb363540fb86a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" /> has no configuration, and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5a94f54f83d0d2dc88f527c47db299a61ffcfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" /> doesn't either. \n</p><p>We now prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/5/875cf935bf2ec7f3d7c99a60f7e50c1afd7dabd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"21\" /> does not work. WLOG, label the origin of our lattice plan with label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. WLOG, we can cover every point that we can with label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> because not labeling any such possible point does not affect the distance between points that aren't allowed to be labelled. \n</p><p>Name the state of the lattice grid before using label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. After covering every point possible, we are left with only lattice points that have coordinates that sum to an odd integer. Notice, however, that if we discard the covered points and rotate all the new points by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/d/11d3791d908578e9ede8da9a29fefa2429468419.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"13\" height=\"33\" /> radians counterclockwise, we end up with the same grid as state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, only all the distances are multiplied by scalar <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/4/2c4dc1409208ff6a3039f4871d85c16763334983.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" />. However, before placing label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s, note that we are in the same state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> as before, because our minimum distance is also multiplied by scalar <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/4/2c4dc1409208ff6a3039f4871d85c16763334983.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" />. Thus, we will always be in an infinite loop, stuck in state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, and thus it is impossible to complete the labeling. \n</p><p>It suffices to show that any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/4/9d4113410ce24198e2caeb26d99781a2bd0c522f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=2^{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"17\" /> works given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/4384d69605e1c4fbd77f71a3eba43c296a1bea69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt;0.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"15\" />. Note that the two smallest distances between any two points in the starting grid are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/4/2c4dc1409208ff6a3039f4871d85c16763334983.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"21\" />. When we are currently in state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> using label <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, it is obvious that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/7/cd72c948bf188a55a5df3d964b04fc86d2fe55bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2ki-i\\le{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" />, we can break out of state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> (Our effective minimum length would be at most 1, and we can cover every remaining point). Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/4384d69605e1c4fbd77f71a3eba43c296a1bea69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt;0.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"15\" />, there does exist an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> where this occurs. Thus, any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/a/4fa5dca8cbf3ead966309f01942a73e0f57cabb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c&lt;\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"21\" /> works, hence proved. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/5/fd55f34d0154b833e4c1570c01cf915587839d3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare{}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> ~SigmaPiE\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017135016\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.030 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.043 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 493/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 19554/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:15580:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017135016 and revision id 195821. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}